Durarara!! AU
Chapter 3 – Part 1
Morning light filtered weakly through the blinds of the net café.
Curtis had never made it to the new flat.
He had walked the city until the trains stopped, then kept walking, letting the residual adrenaline from rooftops, cracked concrete, strange conversations, and two street performances burn itself out under the neon. By the time the sky began to pale, the last train was long gone and the idea of finding the apartment in the dark had felt like more effort than it was worth.
So he had ended up here.
In a small, private booth at a 24-hour net café, he pulled a folded bag from under the chair, unzipped it, and began changing. The gray hoodie, black mask, sunglasses, and Jumpman cap were packed away one piece at a time. In their place came a clean white dress shirt, a blue tie, a matching blue suit jacket, and tailored blue trousers. The long black braids were left free, falling neatly down the front of the jacket on either side.
When he finished, he checked his reflection in the dark screen of the powered-off monitor.
Heterochromatic eyes—one clear green, one sharp blue—stared back at him without the usual barrier of lenses. The black mask was gone. For the first time since landing in Japan, his face was fully visible.
Curtis exhaled and muttered under his breath.
“I’m so stupid. I missed the train last night. Good thing I kept my uniform. Be stupid if I missed the first day… or showed up in my mask.”
He adjusted the blue tie once, smoothed the front of the jacket, and picked up the bag. The net café booth was left empty behind him as he stepped out into the morning streets of Ikebukuro, now looking like any other college student on his way to the first day at Raira—except for the height, the braids, the dark skin, and the mismatched eyes that made more than one passerby glance twice.
Raira College looked cleaner in the daylight than the streets Curtis had walked all night.
He found the correct building, the correct floor, and the correct classroom with minimal trouble, the blue uniform fitting in just enough that no one stopped him. The long black braids and mismatched eyes still drew a few second glances, but most students were too busy with first-day nerves to comment.
He slid into an empty seat near the middle of the room, set his bag down, and folded his arms on the desk.
Within two minutes he was asleep.
The morning proceeded without him.
The introduction ceremony for the new academic year began with the usual formalities. Teachers spoke. Students shifted in their seats. Then the class introductions started.
Mikado Ryūgamine stood first, awkward but earnest, giving his name and a short, polite greeting that somehow still carried a quiet undercurrent of excitement about being in the city. A few seats away, Anri Sonohara rose next. Her voice was soft, almost fragile, as she introduced herself with the same careful restraint she carried in everything. When Rio Kamichika’s turn came, she stood a little stiffly, eyes briefly flicking toward the sleeping figure in the blue uniform before she gave her name in a quieter tone than usual.
The door opened partway through the remaining introductions.
Seiji Yagiri stepped in late, expression already closed off. He didn’t bother sitting down. In front of the entire class and the teacher, he announced that he would not be attending Raira any longer. No long explanation. No apology. Just the flat statement, delivered with the kind of finality that left the room in a brief, uncomfortable silence before he turned and left as abruptly as he had arrived.
The teacher cleared her throat, trying to reclaim control of the morning.
It was only then that she noticed the tall student still asleep at his desk, head resting on his folded arms, long braids spilling over the blue jacket.
She called his name once.
No response.
She called it again, firmer.
Curtis stirred. Heterochromatic eyes—one green, one blue—opened slowly as he lifted his head, blinking against the classroom light. For a second he looked like someone who had forgotten where he was. Then the awareness settled back in.
The teacher gave him a pointed look.
“Since you’re finally with us… please introduce yourself to the class.”
Every set of eyes in the room turned toward the tall transfer student who had slept through half the morning’s events. Rio’s gaze lingered a fraction longer than the others. Mikado looked curious. Anri watched quietly from her seat.
Curtis sat up fully, the blue uniform jacket shifting across his shoulders, and prepared to speak for the first time without a mask.
Curtis sat up straight, the long black braids falling forward over the blue jacket as he faced the room.
His voice was calm and even, carrying easily without effort.
“I’m Curtis Nightstar. Pleasure to be here.”
That was all.
No extra details. No hometown speech. No interests or hobbies. Just the name and the bare minimum of politeness before he settled back into his seat like the introduction had already taken more energy than he wanted to spend.
A brief, awkward silence followed.
A few students exchanged glances. The height, the braids, the mismatched green and blue eyes, and the complete lack of nervous energy made him stand out even in a room full of new faces. Mikado looked openly curious. Anri’s gaze lingered a moment longer than most, quiet and assessing. The teacher gave a small nod, accepting the sparse introduction for what it was, and moved on to the next student.
From her seat, Rio kept her eyes on him a second longer than necessary.
The voice was the same.
The build was the same.
The braids matched what she had seen when the hood had fallen the night before.
She said nothing. She simply looked down at her desk, fingers tightening slightly around her pen, while the rest of the class continued with introductions as if the tall transfer student who had slept through half the morning was nothing more than another new name.
Rio kept her eyes on her desk long after the teacher moved on.
Curtis Nightstar.
The name settled into place with a quiet weight. Hearing it out loud in a classroom made everything from the night before feel sharper. The man who had caught her, spoken to her like her pain wasn’t something to laugh at, then disappeared into a street performance was now sitting a few rows away in a blue uniform like any other student. The braids were the same. The voice was the same. Only the mask was gone.
She didn’t know how to feel about that.
Part of her was relieved he was here—solid, real, not just some strange figure who had appeared when she needed him and vanished. Another part of her felt exposed. He had seen her at her lowest. He knew why she had jumped. And now they shared a classroom. The thought made her grip her pen a little tighter.
He didn’t look at me when he introduced himself.

Maybe that’s better.
She kept her head down and waited for the feeling in her chest to settle.

A few seats away, Anri Sonohara watched the tall transfer student with quiet attention.
She had seen him the night before—first as a masked figure pulling a crowd out of nothing with his voice, then again when the hood fell and the long black braids appeared. The performance had stayed with her longer than most things did. There had been something controlled and heavy in the way he carried the songs, like someone used to keeping everything locked down.
Now the mask was gone.
The face underneath was striking in a different way than she had expected. The mismatched eyes—one green, one blue—gave him an intensity that the sunglasses had previously hidden. He had introduced himself with almost nothing, as if offering more than a name would have been unnecessary. That restraint felt familiar somehow.
Anri’s gaze lowered after a moment, returning to the surface of her desk.
She didn’t know his story.

She only knew that the same person who had stood on a sidewalk and sung about pain and masks was now sitting in her class, looking like he had already decided how much of himself the world was allowed to see.
The thought stayed with her longer than she expected.
Lunch break scattered students across the campus.
Curtis had found a quiet spot near the edge of the courtyard, back against a low wall, eating without much interest. From where he sat he had a clear view of the main path leading away from the buildings.
That was how he noticed the exchange.
Anri Sonohara intercepted Seiji Yagiri before he could fully leave the grounds. Her voice was quiet but insistent as she asked whether he knew anything about a certain girl’s whereabouts. Seiji’s reaction was immediate and sharp. He denied knowing anything, the words edged with clear anger, before turning and walking away without giving her room to press further.
Masaomi Kida stepped in a moment later, concern written plainly across his face as he spoke to Anri. She shook her head, offering a small, dismissive gesture that made it clear she didn’t want the worry. Masaomi lingered for a second longer, then let it go when she insisted she was fine.
Curtis watched the entire thing in silence, mismatched eyes tracking the way Seiji disappeared and the way Anri stood alone for a few seconds afterward before moving on.
He didn’t get long to think about it.
“Curtis?”
The voice came from his left—familiar, quieter than it had been the night before, but steady enough.
Rio Kamichika stood a few steps away, uniform neat, expression carefully controlled. She looked like she had spent the entire morning deciding whether to approach him at all. Now that she had, she didn’t quite seem to know what to do with the silence that followed his name.
She waited, watching him, the courtyard noise continuing around the two of them.
Curtis looked up from where he sat, mismatched green and blue eyes settling on her without surprise.
“Oh. Hey.”
He lifted one hand in a small, casual wave—like they were just two students crossing paths on campus instead of two people who had shared a rooftop, a fall, and a conversation neither of them had planned.
Rio stood there for a second longer than was comfortable, clearly thrown by how normal he made it sound.
“…Hey,” she answered, voice quieter than his. She shifted her weight, fingers adjusting the strap of her bag. “I, um… I didn’t know you went here.”
The words came out careful. Not accusatory. Just the first thing she could manage while standing in front of the same person who had caught her the night before and was now sitting in a blue uniform like the entire thing had been ordinary.
She glanced away for a moment, then back to him.
“You look different without the mask.”
Curtis gave a small nod, the long black braids shifting slightly with the movement.
“Oh. So you did recognize me.” His voice stayed calm, almost casual. “I chose to give you space in class. How are you feeling?”
The directness of the question landed quietly between them.
Rio’s fingers tightened around the strap of her bag. She glanced off to the side for a second, watching other students pass through the courtyard, before looking back at him. The mismatched eyes made it harder to hide behind anything.
“…I’m okay,” she said after a moment. It wasn’t entirely convincing, but it wasn’t a complete lie either. “Better than last night, at least. I went home. I slept. I didn’t… try anything else.”
She hesitated, then added in a lower voice,
“You didn’t have to give me space. But… thank you. For doing it anyway.”
Her gaze dropped briefly to the ground, then returned to his face.
“It still feels weird seeing you here. Like the night version of you and this one are two different people.”
Curtis regarded her for a moment, expression steady.
“I dress the way I do at night to blend with the darkness,” he said. “I’m still me.”
He shifted his weight and closed the small distance between them by half a step—not enough to crowd her, just enough that the words that followed stayed between the two of them.
“I’m not going to say anything to anyone. So don’t worry about anything.” His voice remained low and even. “I’m simply a wanderer.”
The quiet certainty in the statement left little room for doubt. He didn’t soften it with extra reassurances or ask her to trust him. He simply stated it as fact, the same way he had spoken to her on the sidewalk the night before.
Rio looked up at him, searching the mismatched green and blue eyes for any sign that he might be saying it just to make her feel better. She didn’t find any. After a few seconds she gave a small, almost reluctant nod.
“…Okay,” she said quietly. “I believe you.”
She adjusted the strap of her bag again, the tension in her shoulders easing by a fraction.
“Wanderer, huh?” A faint, tired breath that might have been trying to become a smile crossed her face. “That’s one way to put it.”
Curtis gave a small, single nod at her words.
“Yup. A simple one.”
That was all he offered before he turned away.
He didn’t wait for a response or linger to see how she took the answer. He simply started walking, long black braids shifting against the blue uniform jacket as he headed toward the stairwell that led up to the roof. His pace was unhurried, the same steady gait he had used the night before when moving through Ikebukuro’s streets.
Rio remained where she stood for a moment, watching his back as he disappeared into the building.
Curtis took the stairs two at a time without any particular urgency, the sounds of the campus fading behind him with each floor. When he pushed open the door to the roof, the afternoon air met him—open sky, distant city noise, and the quiet that always seemed to settle above the rest of Raira.
He stepped out fully, letting the door close behind him, and walked toward the edge without any clear purpose other than the space itself.
Rio stood alone in the courtyard after he left.
A simple wanderer.
The words kept turning over in her head. He had made everything sound so straightforward—catching her, talking her down, promising silence, then walking away like none of it required further discussion. Part of her was grateful. The other part felt strangely unsettled by how easily he could appear, change something, and disappear again.
She glanced toward the building he had entered.
He’s going to the roof.
The thought came unbidden. She didn’t follow. Not yet. Instead she stayed where she was, arms loosely crossed, trying to decide whether seeing him in the daylight made last night feel more real… or less.

Up on the roof, Curtis pushed the door open and stepped into the open air.
Another student was already there, off to one side, doing their own thing—headphones in, attention elsewhere, clearly not looking for company. Curtis gave them a brief glance and nothing more. He had no interest in interrupting.
He walked to the opposite edge of the roof, where the view opened up over the city, and pulled a cigarette from the pack in his pocket. The lighter clicked once. A thin trail of smoke rose as he took the first drag, mismatched eyes half-lidded against the afternoon light.
“Quiet school,” he muttered under his breath. “Perfect.”
The long black braids shifted slightly in the breeze as he leaned his forearms against the railing, blue uniform jacket open just enough to be comfortable, and let the moment settle around him without further comment.
Rio lasted another few minutes in the courtyard before the decision settled.
She didn’t fully understand why she was going after him. The conversation had already ended. He had given her the reassurance she needed and walked away. Following felt unnecessary. And yet the thought of him up there alone, the same way he had moved through the night like distance was his default setting, pulled at her until she finally moved.
The stairwell was quiet. Each floor she climbed made the sounds of the campus fade a little more. When she pushed open the roof door, the afternoon air met her first—open, cooler, carrying the faint scent of cigarette smoke.
Curtis was exactly where she expected.
He stood at the far edge of the roof, forearms resting against the railing, long black braids hanging forward as he stared out over the city. The cigarette burned slowly between his fingers. He hadn’t turned at the sound of the door. If he noticed her presence, he gave no immediate sign of it.
The other student from earlier was still off to the side, headphones in, completely absorbed in their own world.
Rio hesitated just inside the doorway for a second, then stepped fully onto the roof and let the door close behind her. She didn’t call out. She simply stood there, watching the tall figure in the blue uniform as he continued staring off into the distance like the city below held something only he could see.
The bell rang across the campus, sharp and clear even up on the roof.
Curtis flicked the last of the cigarette over the edge of the railing, watching the ember disappear before he finally turned.
He saw her standing near the door.
For a moment the usual unreadable calm stayed on his face. Then it shifted—just slightly. A gentle smile settled across his features, soft enough that it didn’t feel forced, and he lifted one hand in a small, polite wave.
“C’mon, Rio,” he said, voice easy. “We might be late.”
He didn’t sound surprised that she had followed him up. He simply stepped away from the railing and started walking toward the door, long black braids moving with the motion, as if the two of them heading back down to class together was the most natural next step in the day.
The other student on the roof never looked up from their headphones.
Rio fell into step beside him as they moved toward the stairwell, still a little thrown by the easy smile and the casual wave.
“…You’re very comfortable,” she said after a few steps, glancing at him sideways. “And casual. Like none of this is strange at all.”
She didn’t sound accusing—just quietly observant, the way someone might point out a detail they hadn’t expected. The boy who had caught her off a rooftop, spoken to her like her life still mattered, then disappeared into the night was now walking her back to class with a polite little wave and a comment about being late, as if they had known each other far longer than a single chaotic evening.
Curtis didn’t slow down. The gentle expression hadn’t fully left his face as he reached for the door.
“Should I not be?” he asked simply, holding it open for her without making a show of it.
Rio stepped through the door he held open, then glanced back at him as they started down the stairs.
“…I don’t know,” she admitted after a moment. “Most people would act different. After everything that happened last night, I mean. You saved me, said all those things, then showed up in my class like it was nothing. And now you’re just… smiling and telling me we might be late.”
She kept her voice low so it wouldn’t carry down the stairwell.
“It’s not bad. It’s just unexpected. You’re hard to figure out, Curtis.”
She looked ahead again, one hand on the railing as they descended.
“I guess I’m still getting used to the fact that the person from last night and the one walking next to me are the same.”
Curtis let out a quiet chuckle as they continued down the stairs, the sound low and brief.
“Why’s that? ’Cause I dress different outside school?” He glanced sideways at her, mismatched eyes catching the light from the stairwell window. “Different outfit. Same person.”
Rio was quiet for a couple of steps, considering the simplicity of the answer.
“…It’s not just the clothes,” she said eventually. “Last night you felt… heavier. Like someone who’d already seen too much and decided not to look away. Today you’re smiling and making jokes about being late to class. It’s a big shift.”
She hesitated, then added more softly,
“I’m not saying it’s fake. I just don’t know which version I’m supposed to get used to.”
Her hand stayed on the railing as they reached the next landing, the sounds of the rest of the school starting to filter up from below.
Curtis stopped on the landing.
Before Rio could take another step, he turned and pulled her into a quiet, solid hug. There was nothing dramatic about it—just the steady pressure of someone who meant exactly what he was about to say. The long black braids fell forward slightly as he held her, blue uniform jacket warm against the cooler air of the stairwell.
His voice came low and even near her ear.
“There’s only one. The one that needed no thanks or a grand ceremony. I would save you again with no problem.” A short pause. “You matter, Rio.”
For a second Rio went completely still in his arms.
The words hit harder than she expected. Her hands hovered in the air before slowly coming up to rest against the back of his jacket, fingers curling into the fabric. She didn’t speak right away. When she finally did, her voice was tight and a little unsteady.
“…You can’t just say things like that.”
It wasn’t a rejection. It sounded closer to someone who didn’t know what to do with sincerity when it arrived without conditions. Her forehead rested lightly against his shoulder for a brief moment as she breathed in, then out.
“You’re going to make it really hard to pretend last night didn’t mean anything,” she murmured.
She didn’t pull away immediately.
Curtis gently pulled back from the hug, giving her space without fully stepping away. His hands rested lightly at her shoulders for a second before dropping to his sides. The gentle expression remained, steady and open.
“Well, it did mean something,” he said. “You needed clarity to look at the world. We could have spoken more last night, but you seemed distressed… and the best course of action was to give you space and let you process everything.”
He paused, mismatched eyes holding hers without pressure.
“The fact that we’re in the same academy and class is a win-win.”
Rio stared up at him, still a little off-balance from the hug and the honesty that kept coming without any visible effort on his part. She opened her mouth once, closed it, then tried again.
“…You really think about things like that, don’t you?” she asked quietly. “Most people would’ve either hovered or disappeared completely. You just… decided what I needed and did it.”
A faint, complicated expression crossed her face—part gratitude, part lingering disbelief.
“I’m glad we’re in the same class,” she admitted after a moment. “Even if it’s still a little strange. It’s… nice knowing you’re around.”
The second bell rang faintly from below, reminding them both that time hadn’t actually stopped on the stairwell landing.
 They reached the classroom door just before the final bell.
Curtis glanced over at Rio as he pushed it open.
“Don’t be a stranger, Rio.”
The words were simple, delivered with the same easy calm as everything else he had said on the stairs. Then he stepped inside and headed for his seat.
Rio watched him go for a second longer than necessary.
He seems tired.
The thought settled quietly as she took her own seat. The long night, the missed train, the net café—none of it showed clearly on his face, but there was a heaviness in the way he sat down and immediately folded his arms on the desk.
Class began.
Rio’s observation proved correct within minutes. Curtis’s head lowered, braids spilling forward, and he was asleep again before the teacher had finished taking attendance.
The lesson continued around him.
Near the end of the period, the teacher closed her notebook and looked out over the room.
“We still need a class representative. Any volunteers?”
A brief silence followed. Then Anri Sonohara raised her hand, quiet but clear. The teacher nodded, already starting to write the name down—
—when a second hand went up.
Curtis, still half-asleep, had stretched both arms upward with a quiet groan as he came back to himself. One hand remained in the air just long enough for the teacher to notice.
“Thank you, Curtis,” she said, adding his name beside Anri’s.
Curtis blinked, mismatched eyes focusing slowly.
“…Huh?”
A few students turned to look. Near the middle of the room, Mikado’s hand—which had been halfway up—lowered again. A faint, slightly bothered expression crossed his face at having been beaten to the punch by the transfer student who had been asleep for most of the period.
The bell rang a moment later.
As students began packing up, Anri made her way between the desks and stopped beside Curtis’s seat, waiting for him to fully register that he was now co-class representative with her.
Anri stopped beside his desk, hands resting lightly against the front of her bag. Her expression was as soft and composed as usual, but her eyes were attentive.
“Sonohara Anri,” she introduced herself quietly. “It seems we’re class representatives together.”
She paused for a moment, studying the long black braids and the mismatched eyes that had been hidden behind sunglasses the night before.
“…Were you the one wearing the mask last night?” she asked. “On the street. With the guitar.”
The question was direct, but her tone remained calm—curious rather than accusatory. She waited, giving him the space to answer or deflect as he chose, while the rest of the class continued packing up and filtering out around them.
Curtis leaned back slightly in his chair, mismatched eyes settling on her with mild interest.
“Hmm. What gave it away? My braids?” He reached up and lightly tugged one of the long black plaits forward. “My hood fell off last night. And my braids fell forward.”
Anri gave a small, almost imperceptible nod.
“Yes,” she answered simply. “That… and your voice. The way you carried the songs. It stayed with me.”
She didn’t elaborate or look embarrassed about having watched so closely. Her gaze remained steady, quiet, and a little thoughtful.
“You look different without the mask,” she added after a moment. “But not in a bad way. Just… clearer.”
Around them, the classroom continued to empty. Rio had already left, and most of the other students were filtering into the hallway. Anri made no move to walk away yet, as if the quiet exchange still had a little room left in it.
A faint warmth crept across Curtis’s face before he could stop it. The blush was light, but noticeable against his dark skin as he glanced away for half a second, then back to her.
“Oh… my apologies. I’m not sure what to say.”
The usual calm in his voice had thinned just slightly, replaced by something closer to genuine uncertainty. Being recognized was one thing. Being told he looked clearer without the mask—and having it said so simply—was another.
Anri’s expression softened by a fraction. She didn’t smile widely, but there was a quiet understanding in the way she regarded him.
“You don’t have to say anything,” she replied. “I wasn’t expecting an answer. I just… wanted to know if it was really you.”
She adjusted the strap of her bag, still standing beside his desk.
“The performance was memorable. That’s all.”
Her tone remained gentle, leaving the conversation open without pressing him for more. The classroom had grown quieter around them as the last few students filtered out, leaving the newly appointed class representatives alone in the settling silence.
Curtis scratched lightly at his cheek, the earlier flush still lingering.
“Well… thanks. It felt easier to perform with the mask on.”
Anri considered that for a moment, her gaze moving briefly over the long braids and the now-uncovered face in front of her.
“I liked the mask,” she said quietly. “It suited the way you performed. But…” She paused, choosing the words with her usual care. “Your face is nice too.”
The simple statement landed without any particular emphasis, which somehow made it land harder.
Curtis’s blush returned deeper than before. A small, almost reluctant smile pulled at the corner of his mouth as he looked away for a second, then back to her, mismatched eyes warmer than they had been all day.
“…You’re very direct,” he muttered, the smile still there despite the mild embarrassment.
Anri simply tilted her head a little, as if she hadn’t said anything unusual at all, and continued to stand there in the quiet classroom with him.
Anri’s composure faltered the moment the words left her mouth.
A faint pink rose across her cheeks as she realized how freely she had spoken. She dipped into a quick, flustered bow.
“I—excuse me,” she murmured, already turning. Before Curtis could respond, she slipped out of the classroom with uncharacteristic haste, the door closing softly behind her.
The room was left quiet again.
A moment later, soft footsteps approached from the hallway.
“Curtis-kun?”
Rio stood near the front of the desks, watching him with a slightly uncertain expression. She had clearly been waiting for the other girl to leave before stepping in.
Curtis looked over, the lingering warmth from the previous conversation still faintly visible on his face, and offered a small nod.
“Hey, Rio.”
Rio shifted her weight slightly, fingers adjusting the strap of her bag.
“Are you going home?” she asked.
Curtis shook his head once, long braids moving with the motion.
The small refusal landed with a quiet weight. A flicker of mild disappointment crossed Rio’s face before she could fully hide it. She had clearly been hoping the answer might be yes—maybe even that they could walk part of the way together.
She hesitated for a second longer, then gathered a little more courage.
“…Can I have your number?”
Curtis didn’t pause.
He pulled his phone from his pocket, unlocked it, and held it out toward her without any visible deliberation. The ease of the gesture made the moment feel almost too simple compared to everything else that had passed between them.
Rio’s expression brightened the moment she realized he wasn’t going to question it or make her wait. A small, genuine smile pulled at her lips as she quickly entered her own number and sent herself a message so he would have hers in return.
“Thank you,” she said, quieter but clearly happier than she had been a minute earlier. “I’ll… text you later, if that’s okay.”
She looked down at her phone once, as if confirming the new contact was real, then back up at him with a little less uncertainty than before.  
Curtis slipped his phone back into his pocket, mismatched eyes steady on her.
“It’s fine, Rio. Text me if things get too heavy… or if you just want to talk.”
The offer landed simply, without any extra weight attached to it. He said it the same way he had said everything else since the night before—like it was the obvious next step rather than something that needed ceremony.
Rio’s fingers tightened slightly around her phone. For a second she didn’t answer, as if she needed to make sure she had heard him correctly. Then a quieter, more genuine smile settled across her face.
“…Okay,” she said. “I will.”
She glanced down at the new contact on her screen once more, then back up at him.
“Thank you. For… all of it. Not just the number.”
The hallway outside the classroom had grown mostly empty. She lingered a moment longer, clearly reluctant to end the exchange, before giving him a small nod.
“I’ll see you tomorrow, Curtis-kun.”
  
